    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters August 17 with a high-leverage slate, headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (68.93) and **Austin Riley** (65.33). **Corbin Carroll** is our primary engine today; while the platoon disadvantage against Alec Gamboa is a mild drag, the batting order stability and favorable park factors solidify his role as our most reliable scoring option. **Austin Riley** presents an intriguing value play; despite being projected in the bottom third of the order, he grades out as a top-tier start because he faces Bailey Ober\u2019s generous 4.81 xERA, and the slow delivery profile of the opposing starter provides a marginal uptick for his stolen base probability\u2014a crucial needle-mover in our 5x5 constraints.\n\nThe lineup configuration required difficult choices, particularly regarding **Mickey Moniak** (45.72). His inclusion was heavily scrutinized given his collision with Blake Snell\u2019s elite 2.39 xERA; the decision to start him was driven by a significant park factor boost that offsets his sub-40 raw platoon efficiency. Conversely, our bench remains stagnant due to an off-day for the core trio of **Jose Caballero**, **Josh Naylor**, and **Brice Turang**, while our depth is further thinned by the continued IL absences of **James Wood** and **Trevor Story**. We are monitoring the status of **Gabriel Moreno**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Max Muncy**, and **Seiya Suzuki** closely; all are currently listed with pending lineups. Should any of these players slide down their respective batting orders upon official confirmation, we risk a \"below floor\" efficiency collapse, particularly for **Gabriel Moreno**, whose reliance on that #3 spot is vital to maintaining his 57.83 score. We remain prepared to pivot to the bench reserves\u2014including **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Cole Young**, or **Henry Bolte**\u2014if the final lineups 